http method is the action to be taken by the server and then status code are the response codes of the server to the client browser


Core HTTP Methods

HTTP methods specify the intent of a request. When building APIs, following RESTful conventions requires mapping these methods strictly to CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations

GET	. the operation mostly include to read data .
Retrieves data from a server. It must never modify data

POST . the operation mostly include to create data .
Submits data to be processed, creating a new resource.

PUT	the operation mostly include to update or replace data.
Replaces the entire target resource with the uploaded payload

PATCH	the operation mostly include to update data.
 Applies partial modifications to a resource.

DELETE	the operation mostly include to delete data.	
Destroys the specified resource.



HTTP Status Codes

Status codes are 3-digit integers issued by a server in response to a client's request. 
They are divided into 5 clear numeric blocks.

1xx: Informational (Request received, continuing process)

101 Switching Protocols: The server agrees to switch protocols

2xx: Success

200 OK: The standard success code for GET, PUT, or PATCH requests.
201 Created: The standard success response for a POST request. Confirms a resource was successfully made.
204 No Content: The request succeeded, but there is nothing to send back in the response body. Heavily used for DELETE actions.

3xx: Redirection

301 Moved Permanently: The URL of the requested resource has been changed permanently. The new URL is given in the response.
302 Found / 307 Temporary Redirect: The resource resides temporarily under a different URL.
304 Not Modified: A crucial caching code. Tells the client's browser: The page hasn't changed since you last looked, just read it out of your local cache to save bandwidth

4xx: Client Error

400 Bad Request: The server cannot process the request due to a client-side error
401 Unauthorized: The user lacks valid authentication credentials (e.g., missing or expired JWT tokens)
403 Forbidden: The server understands who you are, but you do not have permission to access the resource. Think: "I know who you are, but you aren't an Admin."
404 Not Found: The server cannot find the requested resource path.
409 Conflict: The request conflicts with the current state of the server (e.g., trying to register a username that is already taken).
429 Too Many Requests: The user has sent too many requests in a given amount of time. This code is triggered by Rate Limiters.

5xx: Server Error (The server failed to fulfill a valid request)

500 Internal Server Error: A generic error message given when an unexpected condition was encountered (e.g., your code threw an unhandled NullPointerException).
502 Bad Gateway: The server, acting as a gateway or Load Balancer, received an invalid response from the upstream backend server.
503 Service Unavailable: The server cannot handle the request because it is down for maintenance or overloaded.
504 Gateway Timeout: The load balancer or reverse proxy timed out waiting for the actual backend server pool to respond.